WEBVTT

00:00:00.343 --> 00:00:02.178
Last video ended with a bouncing ball.

00:00:02.801 --> 00:00:05.024
It's dropped from one hundred and twenty-eight centimetres,

00:00:05.465 --> 00:00:07.408
and each bounce reaches half the height it

00:00:07.449 --> 00:00:07.888
fell from:

00:00:08.570 --> 00:00:09.150
sixty-four,

00:00:09.772 --> 00:00:10.352
thirty-two,

00:00:11.014 --> 00:00:11.494
sixteen,

00:00:11.996 --> 00:00:12.435
and so on.

00:00:13.218 --> 00:00:15.624
After eight bounces, the heights add to one

00:00:15.725 --> 00:00:17.228
hundred and twenty-seven and a half.

00:00:18.153 --> 00:00:20.497
Each new bounce fills exactly half of the

00:00:20.518 --> 00:00:22.903
gap that's left below one hundred and twenty-eight.

00:00:23.606 --> 00:00:25.490
So the total keeps growing, but it can

00:00:25.531 --> 00:00:27.175
never pass one hundred and twenty-eight.

00:00:28.039 --> 00:00:30.892
Can infinitely many bounces really add to a

00:00:31.034 --> 00:00:31.736
finite number?

00:00:32.543 --> 00:00:33.536
And when does that happen?

00:00:34.232 --> 00:00:35.284
Start with the terms.

00:00:35.889 --> 00:00:38.414
Here r is a half, so each bounce

00:00:38.516 --> 00:00:39.657
is half the one before.

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:43.026
Halving again and again makes a number as

00:00:43.148 --> 00:00:44.069
small as you like.

00:00:45.113 --> 00:00:47.378
After ten halvings, a half to the ten

00:00:47.399 --> 00:00:48.882
is less than one thousandth.

00:00:49.666 --> 00:00:51.640
So the terms get closer and closer to

00:00:51.681 --> 00:00:51.982
zero.

00:00:52.688 --> 00:00:54.692
The same is true for any r between

00:00:54.854 --> 00:00:55.896
minus one and one.

00:00:56.900 --> 00:00:58.906
Multiplying by a number smaller than one in

00:00:59.167 --> 00:01:01.956
size shrinks the size by the same fraction

00:01:02.218 --> 00:01:02.738
every time.

00:01:03.742 --> 00:01:05.862
With r equal to minus a half, the

00:01:05.903 --> 00:01:08.884
terms flip sign, but their size still halves,

00:01:09.326 --> 00:01:10.986
so they close in on zero from both

00:01:11.047 --> 00:01:11.326
sides.

00:01:11.996 --> 00:01:12.922
Now the totals.

00:01:13.466 --> 00:01:14.287
From last video,

00:01:14.790 --> 00:01:17.398
S sub n is a, times one minus

00:01:17.599 --> 00:01:18.080
r to the n,

00:01:18.643 --> 00:01:19.866
over one minus r.

00:01:20.570 --> 00:01:21.875
Split that into two parts.

00:01:22.559 --> 00:01:22.699
It's

00:01:23.202 --> 00:01:24.826
a over one minus r,

00:01:25.329 --> 00:01:28.519
minus a over one minus r, times r

00:01:28.581 --> 00:01:28.921
to the n.

00:01:29.644 --> 00:01:30.404
For our series,

00:01:30.946 --> 00:01:33.409
a over one minus r is one hundred

00:01:33.430 --> 00:01:34.090
and twenty-eight,

00:01:34.753 --> 00:01:36.394
and the second part is the gap that's

00:01:36.436 --> 00:01:36.675
left.

00:01:37.458 --> 00:01:38.421
As n grows, r

00:01:39.124 --> 00:01:41.513
to the n shrinks towards zero, so the

00:01:41.534 --> 00:01:42.999
gap shrinks towards zero too.

00:01:43.802 --> 00:01:45.625
The totals get as close to one hundred

00:01:45.647 --> 00:01:48.292
and twenty-eight as you like, without ever passing

00:01:48.333 --> 00:01:48.412
it.

00:01:49.055 --> 00:01:51.221
That value is called the limiting sum,

00:01:51.724 --> 00:01:52.385
S infinity.

00:01:53.048 --> 00:01:55.112
It's the number the totals get closer and

00:01:55.193 --> 00:01:57.617
closer to, as we add more and more

00:01:57.679 --> 00:01:58.059
terms.

00:01:58.741 --> 00:01:58.881
So

00:01:59.425 --> 00:02:02.680
S infinity equals a over one minus r.

00:02:03.385 --> 00:02:05.735
For our series, it's sixty-four over a half,

00:02:06.038 --> 00:02:07.323
which is one hundred and twenty-eight.

00:02:07.947 --> 00:02:10.720
But it only works when r is between

00:02:10.902 --> 00:02:12.247
minus one and one.

00:02:12.871 --> 00:02:14.981
That's often written with the size of r,

00:02:15.304 --> 00:02:16.851
mod r, less than one.

00:02:17.516 --> 00:02:18.883
Now the ball's whole journey.

00:02:19.326 --> 00:02:21.214
It falls one hundred and twenty-eight centimetres.

00:02:21.857 --> 00:02:24.078
After that, every bounce goes up and comes

00:02:24.119 --> 00:02:26.380
back down the same height, so each bounce

00:02:26.421 --> 00:02:27.581
height is travelled twice.

00:02:28.282 --> 00:02:30.429
Those heights have a limiting sum of one

00:02:30.470 --> 00:02:31.212
hundred and twenty-eight.

00:02:31.755 --> 00:02:33.698
So the ball travels one hundred and twenty-eight,

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:36.665
plus two times one hundred and twenty-eight, which

00:02:36.706 --> 00:02:38.329
is three hundred and eighty-four centimetres.

00:02:39.012 --> 00:02:41.810
Infinitely many bounces, but a finite distance.

00:02:42.643 --> 00:02:43.874
What goes wrong otherwise?

00:02:45.002 --> 00:02:46.825
A magic ball that bounced twice as high

00:02:46.845 --> 00:02:48.829
each time would have r equal to two,

00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:51.074
and its totals would grow without bound.

00:02:51.897 --> 00:02:52.780
If r is one,

00:02:53.403 --> 00:02:55.632
every term is a, so the total grows

00:02:55.693 --> 00:02:56.777
by a each time.

00:02:57.578 --> 00:02:59.780
If r is minus one, the terms are

00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:03.142
a, minus a, a, minus a, and the

00:03:03.202 --> 00:03:05.644
totals bounce between a and zero forever.

00:03:06.208 --> 00:03:07.700
They never settle on one number.

00:03:08.587 --> 00:03:10.533
Whenever the size of r is one or

00:03:10.574 --> 00:03:10.834
more,

00:03:11.437 --> 00:03:13.343
r to the n doesn't shrink, so the

00:03:13.384 --> 00:03:14.567
gap never closes.

00:03:15.250 --> 00:03:16.236
There's no limiting sum.

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:17.593
Let's use it.

00:03:18.379 --> 00:03:21.170
Find the limiting sum of twelve, plus four,

00:03:21.493 --> 00:03:23.139
plus four thirds, and so on.

00:03:23.743 --> 00:03:26.110
First find r, from two ratios.

00:03:26.954 --> 00:03:28.901
Four over twelve is a third,

00:03:29.544 --> 00:03:32.154
and four thirds over four is a third.

00:03:32.737 --> 00:03:34.440
The size of r is less than one,

00:03:34.842 --> 00:03:36.546
so a limiting sum exists.

00:03:37.249 --> 00:03:39.057
Write that down before using the formula.

00:03:39.881 --> 00:03:42.590
S infinity is twelve over one minus a

00:03:42.671 --> 00:03:45.801
third, which is twelve over two thirds.

00:03:46.605 --> 00:03:49.276
Dividing by two thirds means multiplying by three

00:03:49.378 --> 00:03:51.024
over two, so it's eighteen.

00:03:51.681 --> 00:03:54.470
When r is negative, the totals zigzag.

00:03:55.052 --> 00:03:58.321
Take eight, minus four, plus two, minus one,

00:03:58.542 --> 00:03:58.903
and so on.

00:03:59.585 --> 00:03:59.886
Here r

00:04:00.589 --> 00:04:01.614
is minus a half.

00:04:02.277 --> 00:04:06.048
The totals go eight, four, six, five, five

00:04:06.068 --> 00:04:06.429
and a half,

00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:09.698
jumping above and below one value, and closing

00:04:09.759 --> 00:04:09.819
in.

00:04:10.724 --> 00:04:13.579
S infinity is eight, over one minus minus

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:13.961
a half.

00:04:14.545 --> 00:04:17.174
That's eight over three halves, which is sixteen

00:04:17.236 --> 00:04:19.363
thirds, about five point three three.

00:04:20.207 --> 00:04:22.410
Here the first term is positive, and a

00:04:22.512 --> 00:04:24.715
negative r adds to the bottom, so the

00:04:24.736 --> 00:04:27.039
limiting sum is smaller than the first term.

00:04:27.695 --> 00:04:31.083
Limiting sums also turn repeating decimals into fractions.

00:04:31.966 --> 00:04:34.610
Zero point two seven two seven, repeating,

00:04:35.172 --> 00:04:39.279
is twenty-seven hundredths, plus twenty-seven ten-thousandths, and so

00:04:39.340 --> 00:04:39.419
on.

00:04:40.202 --> 00:04:42.286
Each piece is one hundredth of the one

00:04:42.307 --> 00:04:43.008
before, so

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:44.552
r is one hundredth.

00:04:45.375 --> 00:04:49.324
Then S infinity is twenty-seven hundredths, over ninety-nine

00:04:49.365 --> 00:04:49.725
hundredths.

00:04:50.367 --> 00:04:54.222
The hundredths cancel, leaving twenty-seven over ninety-nine, which

00:04:54.263 --> 00:04:55.647
simplifies to three elevenths.

00:04:56.471 --> 00:04:59.183
Check: three divided by eleven is zero point

00:04:59.244 --> 00:05:00.992
two seven two seven, repeating.

00:05:01.583 --> 00:05:03.237
Here's a way to remember the condition.

00:05:04.022 --> 00:05:04.783
When the size of r

00:05:05.484 --> 00:05:08.428
is less than one, the terms shrink, so

00:05:08.468 --> 00:05:09.389
the total settles.

00:05:10.131 --> 00:05:12.122
Shrinking ratio, settling sum.

00:05:12.865 --> 00:05:14.953
And the value it settles on is a

00:05:15.294 --> 00:05:16.719
over one minus r.

00:05:19.358 --> 00:05:19.722
Your turn.

00:05:20.527 --> 00:05:24.275
The geometric series ten, plus ten r, plus

00:05:24.336 --> 00:05:26.781
ten r squared, and so on, has a

00:05:26.863 --> 00:05:28.566
limiting sum of forty.

00:05:29.412 --> 00:05:30.016
Find r.

00:05:30.803 --> 00:05:31.911
Pause here to answer.

00:05:42.481 --> 00:05:45.335
Here a is ten, so ten over one

00:05:45.376 --> 00:05:46.742
minus r equals forty.

00:05:47.665 --> 00:05:50.006
Then one minus r is ten over forty,

00:05:50.066 --> 00:05:52.388
which is a quarter, so r is three

00:05:52.448 --> 00:05:52.809
quarters.

00:05:53.590 --> 00:05:56.176
Check the condition: three quarters is between minus

00:05:56.297 --> 00:05:59.003
one and one, so this limiting sum exists.

00:05:59.771 --> 00:06:00.824
Minus three is wrong.

00:06:01.106 --> 00:06:03.112
It turns the fraction upside down, and a

00:06:03.172 --> 00:06:05.299
ratio of minus three has no limiting sum

00:06:05.399 --> 00:06:05.640
at all.

00:06:07.115 --> 00:06:07.565
Last one.

00:06:08.309 --> 00:06:11.091
For what values of x does the geometric

00:06:11.152 --> 00:06:14.876
series one, plus two x, plus four x

00:06:14.977 --> 00:06:17.999
squared, and so on, have a limiting sum?

00:06:18.863 --> 00:06:20.750
Find the limiting sum in terms of x.

00:06:21.897 --> 00:06:23.044
Pause here to answer.

00:06:33.636 --> 00:06:34.943
First find the ratio.

00:06:35.647 --> 00:06:37.415
Each term is two x times the one

00:06:37.456 --> 00:06:39.345
before, so r is two x.

00:06:40.150 --> 00:06:41.820
A limiting sum needs the size of two

00:06:41.942 --> 00:06:42.948
x to be less than one.

00:06:43.472 --> 00:06:45.720
So two x is between minus one and

00:06:45.902 --> 00:06:46.042
one,

00:06:46.545 --> 00:06:48.632
and x is between minus a half and

00:06:48.673 --> 00:06:48.973
a half.

00:06:49.798 --> 00:06:52.292
Then S infinity is one over one minus

00:06:52.313 --> 00:06:52.694
two x.

00:06:53.702 --> 00:06:54.808
Minus one to one is wrong.

00:06:55.272 --> 00:06:57.385
The condition is on the ratio, two x,

00:06:57.848 --> 00:06:58.310
not on x.

00:07:00.074 --> 00:07:03.216
So a geometric series has a limiting sum

00:07:03.617 --> 00:07:05.538
exactly when the size of r is less

00:07:05.579 --> 00:07:06.018
than one,

00:07:06.580 --> 00:07:08.720
and it's a over one minus r.

00:07:09.523 --> 00:07:13.200
Shrinking ratios settle, but growing ratios model real

00:07:13.241 --> 00:07:13.562
growth.

00:07:14.386 --> 00:07:17.032
A country town has twenty thousand people, and

00:07:17.093 --> 00:07:19.238
its population grows by five per cent each

00:07:19.279 --> 00:07:19.458
year.

00:07:20.242 --> 00:07:22.309
How many people live there after six years?

00:07:25.655 --> 00:07:26.538
Here are the key notes.

00:07:27.061 --> 00:07:28.829
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:28.850 --> 00:07:29.331
to copy them.
